---
/**
 * D4. /guides/manhwa-official-english-release — how many manhwa have an
 * official English edition, and the 50 most popular that do.
 */
export const prerender = true
import DataGuide from '../../components/DataGuide.astro'
import DataList from '../../components/DataList.astro'
import { comicsOfCountry } from '../../lib/catalog.js'
import { pathOfTitle } from '../../lib/section.mjs'
import { dataGuide } from '../../lib/guides-registry.js'
import { englishSplit, countPlatforms, notAdult, thumbOf, factLine, num, percent, listWords } from '../../lib/data-guides.mjs'
import homeRules from '../../../data/home-rules.json'

const slug = 'manhwa-official-english-release'
const meta = dataGuide(slug)

const manhwa = comicsOfCountry('KR')
const split = englishSplit(manhwa, { limit: 50, keep: notAdult(homeRules) })
const { rows } = split
const linked = split.english + split.otherOnly
const englishPlatforms = countPlatforms(englishSplit(manhwa, { limit: Infinity }).rows, (row) => row.english)
const inTop = countPlatforms(rows, (row) => row.english)
const top = rows[0]?.item
// The unique banner npm run guides:media assigned this guide (src/lib/guides.mjs assignBanners).
const hero = meta.image ? { src: meta.image, alt: meta.imageAlt || meta.title, wide: meta.wide } : null

const list = rows.map(({ item, english }) => ({
  title: item.title,
  path: pathOfTitle(item),
  cover: thumbOf(item.cover),
  sub: factLine(item),
  platforms: english.map((p) => p.site).join(', '),
}))

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How many manhwa are officially translated into English?',
    a: `${num(split.english)} of the ${num(split.total)} manhwa on manhwaindex have at least one official English edition on record, about ${percent(split.english, split.total)}.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Where can I read manhwa in English legally?',
    a: `${listWords(englishPlatforms.slice(0, 4).map((p) => p.site))} carry the most manhwa in English. Each title page lists every official platform and language for that title.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is my favourite manhwa only in Korean?',
    a: `An English edition needs a licence and a translation, and many series never get one, or get one years later. ${num(split.otherOnly)} manhwa here have official editions only in other languages, mostly Korean.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a fan translation an official English release?',
    a: 'No. Only editions published by the rights holder or a licensed partner count here. Fan scans are not listed on manhwaindex.',
  },
]
---

<DataGuide
  slug={slug}
  title={meta.title}
  description={meta.description}
  category={meta.category}
  keywords={['manhwa in english', 'official english manhwa', 'licensed manhwa', 'manhwa english translation', 'where to read manhwa in english']}
  hero={hero}
  items={rows.map(({ item }) => ({ name: item.title, path: pathOfTitle(item), image: item.cover }))}
  faq={faq}
  minutes={5}
>
  <p>
    Most manhwa are never published in English. Korean apps release thousands of series, and only some are
    licensed and translated by an English publisher. Here is how the numbers look across the
    <strong>{num(split.total)}</strong> manhwa on manhwaindex, and the {rows.length} most popular you can read in
    English on an official platform today.
  </p>

  <h2>The numbers</h2>
  <table class="dg-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Manhwa on manhwaindex</th>
        <th scope="col" class="n">Titles</th>
        <th scope="col" class="n">Share</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>With an official English edition</td>
        <td class="n">{num(split.english)}</td>
        <td class="n">{percent(split.english, split.total)}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Official editions in other languages only</td>
        <td class="n">{num(split.otherOnly)}</td>
        <td class="n">{percent(split.otherOnly, split.total)}</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>No official platform on record</td>
        <td class="n">{num(split.none)}</td>
        <td class="n">{percent(split.none, split.total)}</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Put another way: of the {num(linked)} manhwa with any official platform, {percent(split.english, linked)} can be
    read in English. The rest are available in Korean, and often in Japanese, Chinese, Thai or French, but not yet
    in English.
  </p>

  <h2>How this is counted</h2>
  <p>
    Each manhwa on this site lists the official platforms that publish it, with the language of each edition. A
    title counts as having an English release when at least one of those editions is in English, whether it is a
    webcomic app or a printed book from a publisher such as Yen Press. Only manhwa, Korean comics, are counted here;
    Japanese manga and Chinese manhua are left out. The list below is sorted by popularity on AniList, and titles kept off our
    homepage for adult content are left out of the list but counted in the numbers above.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the English editions are</h2>
  <p>The platforms that carry the most manhwa in English, across the whole catalogue:</p>
  <table class="dg-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Platform</th>
        <th scope="col" class="n">Manhwa in English</th>
        <th scope="col" class="n">In the top {rows.length}</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        englishPlatforms.slice(0, 12).map((p) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{p.site}</td>
            <td class="n">{num(p.count)}</td>
            <td class="n">{num(inTop.find((t) => t.site === p.site)?.count || 0)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The {rows.length} most popular manhwa in English</h2>
  <DataList rows={list} label="The most popular manhwa with an official English edition" platformsLabel="In English on" />

  <p>
    Looking for something specific? Every manhwa page on this site shows which languages it is officially
    available in. For the apps themselves, see <a href="/where-to-read">where to read manhwa</a> or the
    <a href="/guides/platforms-with-most-licensed-manga">platforms with the most licensed titles</a>.
  </p>
</DataGuide>
